Apple's challenge to a UK government demand for access to encrypted customer data can now be heard largely in public, after a tribunal rejected the Home Office's attempt to keep the order secret, according to the <a href="https://www.ft.com/content/46a5cf3c-a827-477f-b72f-e7415565e729?syn-25a6b1a6=1"><em>Financial Times</em></a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2025/02/iCloud-Versus-UK-Key-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-988727" /><br/>
The Investigatory Powers Tribunal today ruled that the British government had failed to demonstrate that confirming the demand would damage national security. The decision requires the Home Office to acknowledge that it served Apple with a technical capability notice (TCN), which is an order that can compel companies to make customer data accessible to UK security services, even when it is encrypted.<br/>
<br/>
The government's position relied on its policy of neither confirming nor denying such notices. However, the tribunal found that disclosures to the media, including the <em>Financial Times</em>, and discussions with U.S. officials had already departed significantly from that policy.<br/>
<br/>
In its judgment, the tribunal said the combined effect was that anyone following the dispute already knew Apple had received a TCN. It described the suggestion that official acknowledgment would threaten national security or give a malicious actor fresh information as "fanciful."<br/>
<br/>
The ruling means that much of the legal action brought by Apple, alongside a <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/03/14/activist-groups-challenge-uk-apple-backdoor/">separate challenge</a> from Privacy International and Liberty, can proceed in open court. <br/>
<br/>
Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/03/apple-legal-challenge-against-uk-demand/">filed its latest legal challenge</a> in July, following a second TCN. The Home Office had previously <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/08/19/uk-withdraws-icloud-user-data-demand/">agreed to withdraw</a> an earlier demand covering UK and U.S. customers after a dispute with the American government. The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/10/01/uk-issues-new-order-for-icloud-data/">replacement notice</a> is believed to focus on UK users specifically.<br/>
<br/>
The dispute led Apple to <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2025/02/21/apple-pulls-encrypted-icloud-security-feature-uk/">stop offering Advanced Data Protection</a> to new users in the UK in February 2025. The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/icloud/">iCloud</a> feature provides end-to-end encryption for additional categories of data, including device backups, photos, and notes, meaning Apple does not hold the keys needed to decrypt them.<br/>
<br/>
UK users who had not already enabled ADP can no longer turn it on. Apple <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-gb/122234">says it cannot switch the feature off on their behalf</a>, so it can remain active on accounts where users previously enabled it and have not turned it off. The company said affected users will be given a period to disable ADP themselves to continue using &zwnj;iCloud&zwnj;, but a deadline has still not been provided.<br/>
<br/>
Without ADP, those additional categories of &zwnj;iCloud&zwnj; data use standard protection, under which Apple holds the encryption keys and can provide data in response to valid legal demands. iMessage and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/facetime/">FaceTime</a> remain end-to-end encrypted, as do categories protected by default, such as passwords and Health data. <br/>
<br/>
The tribunal will now be able to hear much of the arguments over the government's encryption demands in public, with the legality of those demands still to be decided. Privacy International <a href="https://privacyinternational.org/legal-action/pi-apple-tcn-challenge/">lists the substantive hearing</a> for its separate challenge as scheduled for December 2026.<div class="linkback">Tags: <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-privacy/">Apple Privacy</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/lawsuit/">Apple Lawsuits</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/united-kingdom/">United Kingdom</a></div><br/>This article, &quot;<a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/19/uk-loses-secrecy-fight-over-apple-backdoor-order/">UK Loses Secrecy Fight Over Apple &#039;Backdoor&#039; Order</a>&quot; first appeared on <a href="https://www.macrumors.com">MacRumors.com</a><br/><br/><a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/uk-loses-secrecy-fight-over-apple-backdoor-order.2494805/">Discuss this article</a> in our forums<br/><br/>